; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.rever.co/\",\"lineno\":55}); Total lines: 374\nL20: #### Discover\nL21: \nL22: Your search for the best roads, race tracks and off-road trails ends here with a motorcycle trip planner built for adventure. Uncover more than 3,000 motorcycle road trip maps around the world with the REVER motorcycle route app.\nL23: \nL24: cite7†get the app†rever.app.link L25: \nL26: #### Track\nL27: \nL28: Record your activities anywhere in the world with an accurate motorcycle ride tracker. Ride tracking feature requires no cell service.\nL29: \nL30: cite12†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com cite13†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com L31: ## Route Planning Made Easy\nL32: \nL33: The REVER motorcycle route app highlights every epic road for easy reference. Create the perfect ride with a motorcycle route planner and automatically sync it to the REVER App for easy navigation and sharing.\nL34: \nL35: cite7†get the app†rever.app.link L36: \nL37: cite14†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com L38: \nL39: cite15†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com cite16†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com L40: ## Navigate\nL41: \nL42: Your expensive GPS has met its match. Get everything you need in a motorcycle GPS app and more like offline maps, turn-by-turn directions, and voice guidance to get you where you're going.\nL43: \nL44: ## Socialize\nL45: \nL46: Live Friend Tracking, Challenges, and much more to keep you motivated, safe and connected while exploring motorcycle riding routes near you.\nL47: \nL48: cite17†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com cite18†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com L49: # The Word Is Out\nL50: \nL51: Never ride alone again with live friend tracking, challenges and more. Stay motivated, safe and connected with the REVER moto trip planner.\nL52: \nL53: cite19†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com L54: \nL55: cite20†Image†cdn.prod.website-files.com L56: love tracking my rides with Rever. This app does this and a heck of lot more. There is a ton of single track moto rides where I live and the tracking system/gps is very accurate. I like the new feature of auto pause and of course I like to see how fast I ride as this app gives me my highest mph which I can compare with my friends. Before Rever came along I would use Map my ride but this was not a motorcycle specific app. Now I track all of my rides and can share them with my friends. Great app!\nL57: #### KTMColorado\nL58: I had planned over 20,000 kms of rides after I had discovered this app. The wife and I are riding 29 states and 3 provinces after I complete a 24 border to border run to Mexico. Planning the trip was easy and with the Butler map overlay completely changed the route we are taking. I was having no luck with the mobile app however... Turns out the email address I used to sign up for facebook was used to log in. Hadn't used that one in years. The found it and it works awesome now! Wicked customer service!\nL59: #### Charles Lott\nL60: \nL61: I love this app, there response to questions and issues are excellent, very prompt and courteous.\nL62: \nL63: #### Shawn Peters\nL64: \nL65: I ride everyday and this App helps me track my milage, great app...\nL66: \nL67: #### Steven Pena\nL68: \nL69: I like the record ride feature & I can easily create rides on website, and then use the app on my phone to ride the route.\nL70: #### Dave Dyczewski\nL71: \nL72: The answer then, to my initial question of \"Have they really made it better?\" would be yes. And as an alternative to other, motorcycle-specific maps, my wife Judy, who is our chief navigator, spent a lot of time comparing several and concluded that the Butler maps are the best.\nL73: \nL74: #### Ken Bingenheimer\nL75: \nL76: If there is a road not on the map it's probably not worth riding.\nL77: \nL78: ‍\nL79: \nL80: #### Motorcyclist Magazine\nL81: \nL82: The word from the navigator is very clear: The Butler Maps Colorado map is the best.\nL83: #### Ken Bingenheimer\nL84: \nL85: # Best Offroad Trails\nL86: \nL87: cite8†Nevada BDR L88: \nL89: cite8†Mid-Atlantic BDR L90: \nL91: cite8†Idaho BDR L92: \nL93: cite8†S. California BDR L94: \nL95: cite8†New Mexico BDR L96: \nL97: cite8†Washington BDR L98: \nL99: cite8†Arizona BDR L100: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRoadRUNNER Motorcycle Touring & Travel Magazine (https://www.roadrunner.travel/)\nciteturn18view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.roadrunner.travel/\",\"lineno\":30}); Total lines: 111\nL24: ## cite3†Motorcycles L25: \nL26: cite18†Motorcycle Review 2026 BMW R 1300 RT It’s for those who want 145 horsepower and heated grips. For those who want to drag a knee, fully loaded with luggage and pillion. BMW set out to redefine sport-touring with the release of the 2026 R 1300 RT. This year, “RT” stands for “refine things,” as BMW 8 min read\nL27: cite19†WEB Exclusive Premium Content Yamaha WR125R: Two Paths, One Adventure Spend enough time around motorcyclists, and you'll hear a familiar refrain: bigger engines, longer suspension, more horsepower, more capability. By that logic, the Yamaha Ténéré 700 has become a benchmark middleweight adventure motorcycle.  It lets you devour interstate miles with comfort, carve mountain passes, and confidently tackle rough 5 min read\nL28: cite20†Motorcycle Review 2026 Suzuki GSX-8TT Any journo’s self-assigned knee-jerk task when reviewing a motorcycle is to point out its flaws. While this is a valid function of a bike review, doing so with the Suzuki GSX-8TT can feel a bit silly. Especially when you’d be relegated to complaining about petty 6 min read\nL29: \nL30: * * *\nL31: ## cite21†Destinations L32: \nL33: cite11†Destinations The Crossroads of Great Riding—Cashiers, NC Cashiers is a small North Carolina town near the state’s southern border with a population of only a few hundred people. And it’s one of the